20.4  systemd targets

TL;DR
  - A target is a stage the system can reach. multi-user.target: text mode with
    network and services. graphical.target: the same plus a login screen.
  - Boot stops at the default target: systemctl get-default shows it,
    set-default changes it.
  - rescue.target and emergency.target are minimal modes for repairs.

      systemctl get-default
      sudo systemctl set-default multi-user.target
      sudo systemctl isolate rescue.target   # switch now

On your machine
  Firmware  UEFI · Secure Boot on
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64
  Cmdline   BOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.12.48-amd64
            root=UUID=3f1c2a9e-8b7d-4e21-9c55-0a6b2d7e9f10 ro rootflags=subvol=@
            quiet splash
  Init      systemd · default target graphical.target
  Loader    systemd-boot 256.4 · 1 unified kernel image(s)
  Up        3 days, 4 h

Pitfalls
  - isolate stops every unit the new target doesn't need: from a desktop, going
    to multi-user.target closes your session.
  - For a single boot, add systemd.unit=rescue.target to the kernel command line
    from the boot menu (see 20.2).
  - Rescue and emergency modes ask for the root password. Where root is locked
    (Ubuntu), they can't open a shell.

Deep dive

  ▾ How do targets replace runlevels?

    SysV init had numbered runlevels, 0 to 6, with fixed meanings. Targets have
    names, depend on each other and can be combined. Aliases such as
    runlevel3.target (multi-user) and runlevel5.target (graphical) keep old
    habits working.

  ▾ Rescue or emergency?

    rescue.target mounts the filesystems, starts a few basic services and opens
    a root shell. emergency.target mounts only the root filesystem, read-only,
    for when even /etc/fstab is broken.

Related
  Commands  systemctl
  Paths     /etc/systemd/system/default.target
  Hub       atfq boot Boot
  See also  atfq 22.1 Units and timers
  See also  atfq 20.2 The kernel command line
